-- =====================================================================
--  Seven Rooms, One Key
--
--  ONE COLLECTION. G major, one sharp — G A B C D E F#. Not a single
--  note outside it is sounded anywhere in the piece. What moves instead
--  is the TONAL CENTRE, so each section is a different room in the same
--  house and no simultaneity anywhere can be wrong:
--
--      C lydian    C D E F# G A B      bright, floating   (the #4 shimmer)
--      A dorian    A B C D E F# G      neutral, in motion (natural 6)
--      B phrygian  B C D E F# G A      the darkest usable (b2 over the drone)
--      E aeolian   E F# G A B C D      plain minor        (the crisis)
--      G major     G A B C D E F#      home, arrived last
--
--  Note that on one sharp the phrygian centre is B, not E: E is aeolian
--  here, and writing `scale E phrygian` would smuggle an F natural into
--  the piece and break the whole premise on its first bar. F# locrian is
--  the one centre this collection cannot bear, and it is never used.
--
--  THE THEME ("the Ring") is written in scale degrees and never in note
--  names, so one `scale` line re-colours every interval in it while its
--  contour survives intact:
--
--      degrees   1  5  6 | 4  3  2  0
--      rhythm    2  2  3 | 2  2  1  2      (fourteen eighths, 2+2+3)
--
--  Its signature is the rising fifth 1->5. That fifth is perfect in
--  lydian, dorian, phrygian and ionian — and a TRITONE in aeolian,
--  because degrees 1 and 5 of E on one sharp are F# and C, the only
--  tritone the collection owns. The theme therefore breaks of its own
--  accord at exactly one point in its own key, and `crux` is that point.
--  Nothing is added to the note-set to do it.
--
--  ONE PULSE, MANY BARS. bpm 84 holds from the first bar to the coda, so
--  the eighth note is 168/min in every room: 7/8, 4/4 and 5/8 are one
--  heartbeat measured out in different lengths of bar. The theme in 4/4
--  is a true augmentation — quarters where it had eighths — rather than
--  merely a slower playing of it, and in 5/8 a true compression. Only
--  `gate` modulates, to 56, where the new eighth is the old triplet.
--
--  THE SEVEN-CLOCK. `clock` plays (3,7) in every room. In 7/8 that is
--  three strokes on the eighths, 3+2+2 — the theme's own 2+2+3 rotated.
--  In 4/4 and 5/8 the same figure re-grids into septuplets and drifts
--  against the barline. One shape, four metres, exactly like the theme.
--
--  THE GENERATED LINES. `wing` and `brand` are seeded walks (§3.19). The
--  walk is hashed on the line's NAME, so the name is not a label here —
--  it is part of the composition, chosen by auditioning candidates and
--  keeping the two whose phrases had the best shape. Both are bounded to
--  degrees that keep them inside the room they are playing in.
-- =====================================================================

meta title    "Seven Rooms, One Key"
meta composer "Claude"
meta comment  "One diatonic collection — G major, one sharp — with a moving tonal centre: C lydian, A dorian, B phrygian, E aeolian, home to G. The theme is written in scale degrees so every return re-colours it, and is heard in 7/8, 4/4 and 5/8 against a single constant eighth-note pulse."

bpm 84
sig 7/8
scale C lydian
seed 7
tail 9.0

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
--  Instruments
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Struck glass. A sine fundamental with two genuinely inharmonic bar
-- partials — 2.76 and 5.40 are the free-free bar modes — each on its own
-- shorter envelope, so the clang dies away before the pitch does. The
-- theme's voice in the bright rooms.
def glass {
    voice     poly 10 replaceoldest
    lifecycle oneshot
    tone      sine gain 0.62 identity
    tone sine ratio 2.76 {
        env attack  linear 0 0.18 0.001
        env decay   linear 0.18 0 0.85
        env sustain constant 0
        env release constant 0
    }
    tone sine ratio 5.4 {
        env attack  linear 0 0.08 0.001
        env decay   linear 0.08 0 0.40
        env sustain constant 0
        env release constant 0
    }
    tone      triangle gain 0.13 harmonic 2
    mix       sum
    env       adsr 0.002 1.9 0.0 0.65
    lpf       5200
    gain      0.72
    velsens   0.85
}

-- The pad. Two triangles four cents apart plus a quiet second harmonic
-- and a whisper of sub: almost no odd-harmonic bite, so it sits under
-- everything without fighting the lead for the middle. The tremolo is
-- one sweep per sixteen seconds — felt as breathing, not heard as an
-- effect.
def haze {
    voice     poly 12 replaceoldest
    lifecycle gated
    tone      triangle gain 0.42 identity
    tone      triangle gain 0.30 ratio 1.004
    tone      sine gain 0.16 harmonic 2
    tone      saw gain 0.07 ratio 0.4995
    mix       sum
    env       adsr 1.10 0.90 0.62 1.60
    lpf       1500
    trem      0.062hz 0.18
    gain      0.55
    velsens   0.60
}

-- A hollow reed: odd harmonics only, 1 3 5 7 in a falling series, which
-- is the clarinet's spectrum and reads as woody and covered rather than
-- bright. Carries the theme in the dark rooms and doubles it an octave
-- below the glass in the bright ones.
def hollow {
    voice     poly 6 replaceoldest
    lifecycle gated
    tone      sine gain 0.55 identity
    tone      sine gain 0.26 harmonic 3
    tone      sine gain 0.11 harmonic 5
    tone      sine gain 0.05 harmonic 7
    tone      triangle gain 0.10 ratio 1.002
    mix       sum
    env       adsr 0.055 0.25 0.70 0.45
    lpf       2600
    gain      0.68
    velsens   0.90
}

-- The drone. Monophonic on purpose: one pedal note at a time, replaced
-- rather than layered, so the bottom of the piece can never turn to mud.
-- Filtered at 420 Hz — it is a floor, not a voice.
def moor {
    voice     mono replace
    lifecycle gated
    tone      sine gain 0.80 identity
    tone      sine gain 0.22 harmonic 2
    tone      triangle gain 0.10 harmonic 3
    mix       sum
    env       adsr 0.35 0.60 0.78 1.40
    lpf       420
    gain      0.75
    velsens   0.50
}

-- Plucked. The noise tone is the fingernail: eighteen milliseconds of
-- it, gone before the string has properly spoken, which is what makes a
-- pluck read as a pluck and not as a short organ note.
def thread {
    voice     poly 12 replaceoldest
    lifecycle oneshot
    tone      triangle gain 0.50 identity
    tone      sawraw gain 0.15 identity
    tone      sine gain 0.17 harmonic 4
    tone noise {
        env attack  linear 0 0.10 0.0005
        env decay   linear 0.10 0 0.018
        env sustain constant 0
        env release constant 0
    }
    mix       sum
    env       adsr 0.004 0.55 0.0 0.28
    lpf       3400
    gain      0.60
    velsens   1.00
}

-- The seven-clock. Tubular-bell partials — 2.01, 3.02, 4.17, 5.43 —
-- high-passed so only the shimmer survives and it never competes for
-- the middle of the mix.
def chime {
    voice     poly 8 replaceoldest
    lifecycle oneshot
    tone      sine gain 0.34 identity
    tone      sine gain 0.22 ratio 2.01
    tone      sine gain 0.14 ratio 3.02
    tone      sine gain 0.09 ratio 4.17
    tone      sine gain 0.06 ratio 5.43
    mix       sum
    env       adsr 0.001 1.25 0.0 0.50
    hpf       520
    gain      0.40
    velsens   1.00
}

-- Frame drum. Two fixed low sines plus a pink-noise slap, pitched in
-- hertz rather than by note so it stays exactly where it is whichever
-- room the piece has moved to.
def skin {
    voice     poly 4 replaceoldest
    lifecycle oneshot
    tone      sine freq 74 gain 0.85
    tone      sine freq 111 gain 0.28
    tone      triangle freq 168 gain 0.12
    tone pinknoise {
        env attack  linear 0 0.26 0.0008
        env decay   linear 0.26 0 0.055
        env sustain constant 0
        env release constant 0
    }
    mix       sum
    env       adsr 0.002 0.42 0.0 0.16
    lpf       900
    gain      0.70
    velsens   1.00
}

-- Air. Not a note — a slow band of noise, used twice, to cover a seam.
def dust {
    voice     poly 2 replaceoldest
    lifecycle gated
    tone      pinknoise gain 0.70
    tone      noise gain 0.16
    mix       sum
    env       adsr 1.40 1.00 0.50 2.20
    hpf       300
    lpf       1600
    gain      0.45
    velsens   0.80
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
--  I. dawn — C lydian, 7/8, 35s.
--     The theme stated bare over its own drone. One line enters every
--     other pair of bars and nothing is varied yet; the pad is G-D-E
--     over a C, which is a major ninth with the third on top and no F#
--     in it at all — the shimmer is saved for the melody.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

section dawn 14 {
  bpm 84
  sig 7/8
  scale C lydian

  pedal moor   "c2@6 ~@1"                                       | gain 0.90 | pan -0.12
  air   haze   "[g3,d4,e4]@4 ~@3"                    @ 3..      | gain 0.60 | reverb 0.52 | pan 0.15
  ring  glass  "[1@2 5@2 6@3 4@2 3@2 2@1 0@2]/2"     @ 5..      | oct 1 | vel 0.62 | reverb 0.42 | delay 0.714 0.26 0.20 | pan 0.20
  clock chime  "<4 6 2 4>(3,7)"                      @ 7..      | oct 1 | vel 0.30 | reverb 0.60 | pan -0.45
  harp  thread "~ 4 ~ 6 ~ 2 ~"                       @ 11..     | vel 0.40 | reverb 0.35 | pan 0.42
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
--  II. drift — A dorian, 7/8, 40s.
--     Same contour, new room: the leap 1->5 now lands on F#, which is
--     dorian's characteristic sixth, and the fall 2->0 lands on A. The
--     fourteen-eighth phrase is answered by its own strict melodic
--     inversion — 5 1 0 2 3 4 6, every degree-step negated — in exactly
--     the rhythm it had, so bars 3-4 are bars 1-2 turned upside down.
--     The drone moves for the first time: A for four eighths, a breath,
--     then E for two, which turns the pad from Am6/9 into Em7 under it.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

section drift 16 {
  bpm 84
  sig 7/8
  scale A dorian

  pedal moor   "a1@4 ~@1 e2@2"                                  | gain 0.90 | pan -0.12
  air   haze   "[a3,e4,f#4]@4 [g3,d4,e4]@3"                     | gain 0.58 | reverb 0.52 | pan 0.15
  ring  glass  "[1@2 5@2 6@3 4@2 3@2 2@1 0@2 5@2 1@2 0@3 2@2 3@2 4@1 6@2]/4"  | vel 0.66 | reverb 0.42 | delay 0.714 0.26 0.20 | pan 0.22
  under hollow "[4@6 2@4 1@4]/2"                     @ 5..      | oct -1 | vel 0.50 | lpf 1600 | reverb 0.30 | pan -0.30
  clock chime  "<4 6 2 4>(3,7)"                                 | oct 1 | vel 0.30 | reverb 0.60 | pan -0.45
  harp  thread "~ 4 ~ 6 ~ 2 ~"                       @ 3..      | vel 0.42 | reverb 0.35 | pan 0.42

  group kit {
    drum skin  "X ~ ~ x ~ x ~"                       @ 9..      | vel 0.50 | pan -0.05
    tick rim   "~ ~ x ~ ~ ~ x"                       @ 11..     | vel 0.32 | pan 0.50
  } | lpf 7000 | gain 0.88
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
--  III. shade — B phrygian, 4/4, 34s.
--     The metre changes and the pulse does not, so the theme is heard in
--     exact augmentation: quarters where it had eighths, sixteen beats
--     where it had fourteen, the apex given the two extra. The reed
--     takes it. The pad is kept strictly below the melody — Bm7, then
--     Am7 over the B drone, both voiced without the phrygian second, so
--     that C arrives only in the tune, where it belongs. `glow` tolls a
--     single high B once every four bars.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

section shade 12 {
  bpm 84
  sig 4/4
  scale B phrygian

  pedal moor   "b1@3 ~@1"                                       | gain 0.92 | pan -0.10
  air   haze   "[b3,f#4,a4]@2 [a3,e4,g4]@2"                     | gain 0.60 | reverb 0.58 | pan 0.12
  ring  hollow "[1@2 5@2 6@4 4@2 3@2 2@2 0@2]/4"                | vel 0.62 | lpf 1400..3000 | ramp 12 exp | reverb 0.35 | pan -0.18
  glow  glass  "0/4"                                 @ 5..      | oct 1 | vel 0.30 | reverb 0.62 | pan 0.35
  clock chime  "<0 4 2 5>(3,7)"                                 | vel 0.24 | reverb 0.62 | pan -0.45
  drum  skin   "X ~ ~ ~"                             @ 3..      | vel 0.44 | pan 0.00
  wash  dust   "x/2"                                 @ 9..      | vel 0.30 | gain 0.45 | reverb 0.55
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
--  IV. crux — E aeolian, 4/4, 23s.
--     The one room the theme cannot stand up in. Degrees 1 and 5 of E on
--     one sharp are F# and C, so the rising fifth that has opened every
--     statement so far collapses into a tritone. Only the head survives,
--     struck four times over an E drone while the filter opens on it;
--     the glass answers a bar behind with the same two notes an octave
--     up, and the delay makes a third of them. Em9 to Cadd9 beneath.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

section crux 8 {
  bpm 84
  sig 4/4
  scale E aeolian

  pedal moor   "e1@3 ~@1"                                       | gain 0.95 | pan -0.10
  air   haze   "[e3,b3,d4]@2 [c4,g4,d5]@2"                      | gain 0.62 | reverb 0.50 | pan 0.12
  ring  hollow "[1@2 5@2 6@4]/2"                                | vel 0.72 | lpf 900..3400 | ramp 8 exp | reverb 0.28 | pan -0.20
  echo  glass  "[~@4 1@2 5@2]/2"                     @ 3..      | oct 1 | vel 0.40 | reverb 0.50 | delay 0.714 0.30 0.25 | pan 0.35
  low   thread "0 ~ 2 ~"                             @ 5..      | oct -1 | vel 0.45 | lpf 1200 | reverb 0.25 | pan -0.42

  group kit {
    drum skin  "X ~ x ~"                                        | vel 0.55..0.85 | ramp 8 | pan 0.00
    hats hihat "x*4 x*8"                             @ 5..      | vel 0.28 | pan 0.45
  } | lpf 7000 | gain 0.88
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
--  V. press — A dorian, 5/8, 29s. Played twice: here, and again as a
--     ritornello the moment the solo ends.
--     The bar is cut from seven eighths to five with the pulse left
--     alone, so the theme is compressed rather than hurried: fourteen
--     eighths become ten and the 2+2+3 opening is squeezed to 1+2+2.
--     The same notes, with no room left in them.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

section press 16 {
  bpm 84
  sig 5/8
  scale A dorian

  pedal moor   "a1@3 e2@2"                                      | gain 0.90 | pan -0.10
  pulse thread "0 4 6 4 3"                                      | oct -1 | vel 0.45 | lpf 1800..4200 | ramp 8 exp | reverb 0.20 | pan -0.35
  air   haze   "[a3,e4,g4]@3 [b3,f#4,d5]@2"                     | gain 0.50 | reverb 0.42 | pan 0.20
  ring  hollow "[1@1 5@2 6@2 4@1 3@1 2@1 0@2]/2"     @ 5..      | vel 0.70 | lpf 2400 | reverb 0.25 | pan 0.15
  clock chime  "<4 6 2 4>(3,7)"                      @ 3..      | oct 1 | vel 0.28 | reverb 0.55 | pan -0.45

  group kit {
    drum skin  "X ~ x ~ x"                                      | vel 0.62 | pan 0.00
    hats hihat "x:0.45 x:0.22 x:0.38 x:0.22 x:0.30"             | pan 0.40
    tick rim   "~ ~ x ~ ~"                           @ 7..      | vel 0.35 | pan -0.50
  } | lpf 7000 | gain 0.88
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
--  VI. solo — A dorian, 5/8, 29s. Written question, generated answer.
--     The reed asks with the theme's HEAD (1 5 6) for four bars and with
--     its TAIL (4 3 2 0) for four more; between them the glass answers
--     with a seeded walk of four notes to the bar, which against a bar
--     of five eighths never lines up with the ostinato twice running.
--     The walk is bounded to degrees -3..10 — E4 to D6 — so it cannot
--     leave the room, and its cadence rule pulls each bar's last note
--     toward the centre of that span, which here is E: the fifth of A
--     dorian, so the answers keep settling onto a reciting tone.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

section solo 16 {
  bpm 84
  sig 5/8
  scale A dorian

  pedal moor   "a1@3 e2@2"                                      | gain 0.88 | pan -0.10
  pulse thread "0 4 6 4 3"                                      | oct -1 | vel 0.42 | lpf 2600 | reverb 0.20 | pan -0.35
  air   haze   "[a3,e4,g4]@3 [b3,f#4,d5]@2"                     | gain 0.46 | reverb 0.45 | pan 0.20

  callA hollow "[1@2 5@2 6@1 ~@5]/2"                 @ 1..4     | vel 0.68 | reverb 0.30 | pan 0.10
  wing  glass  "solo(-3..10, 4)"                     @ 5..12    | vel 0.55 | reverb 0.40 | delay 0.357 0.24 0.20 | pan 0.30
  callB hollow "[4@2 3@2 2@1 0@5]/2"                 @ 13..16   | vel 0.68 | reverb 0.30 | pan 0.10

  group kit {
    drum skin  "X ~ x ~ x"                                      | vel 0.55 | pan 0.00
    hats hihat "x:0.40 x:0.20 x:0.34 x:0.20 x:0.28"             | pan 0.40
  } | lpf 7000 | gain 0.88
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
--  VII. flight — A dorian, 5/8, 21s. The walk alone, densifying.
--     One `ramp 12` governs the whole section, so the walk thickens from
--     four notes a bar to eleven while its velocity rises with it, the
--     pluck opens from 1.6 kHz to 5 kHz and the drum grows underneath —
--     a single twelve-bar gesture with nothing else in it. The tune is
--     absent for the first time in the piece; only its room remains.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

section flight 12 {
  bpm 84
  sig 5/8
  scale A dorian

  pedal moor   "a1@3 e2@2"                                      | gain 0.90 | pan -0.10
  pulse thread "0 4 6 4 3"                                      | oct -1 | vel 0.42..0.60 | lpf 1600..5000 | ramp 12 exp | reverb 0.20 | pan -0.35
  air   haze   "[a3,e4,g4]@3 [b3,f#4,d5]@2"                     | gain 0.48 | reverb 0.45 | pan 0.20
  brand glass  "solo(-3..10, 4..12)"                            | ramp 12 | vel 0.45..0.75 | reverb 0.38 | delay 0.357 0.26 0.22 | pan 0.30
  lift  chime  "<4 6 2 4>(3,7)"                      @ 5..      | oct 1 | vel 0.30 | reverb 0.60 | pan -0.45

  group kit {
    drum skin  "X ~ x ~ x"                                      | vel 0.50..0.80 | ramp 12 | pan 0.00
    hats hihat "x:0.28 x:0.16 x:0.24 x:0.16 x:0.22"             | pan 0.40
    tick rim   "~ ~ x ~ ~"                           @ 5..      | vel 0.35 | pan -0.50
  } | lpf 7000 | gain 0.88
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
--  VIII. crest — C lydian, 4/4, 46s. The home colour, augmented, in
--     three real parts: the glass has the theme, the reed doubles it an
--     octave below, and the plucked line runs the inversion underneath
--     it. The two outer lines cross at F# — the lydian fourth, the sharp
--     the whole piece is named for — and land a major seventh apart over
--     the C drone. The pad finally admits the F# itself, alternating
--     G-D-E with G-D-F# every two beats.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

section crest 16 {
  bpm 84
  sig 4/4
  scale C lydian

  pedal moor    "c2@3 g2@1"                                     | gain 0.95 | pan -0.10
  air   haze    "[g3,d4,e4]@2 [g3,d4,f#4]@2"                    | gain 0.62 | reverb 0.50 | pan 0.15
  ring  glass   "[1@2 5@2 6@4 4@2 3@2 2@2 0@2]/4"               | oct 1 | vel 0.75 | reverb 0.40 | delay 0.714 0.24 0.18 | pan 0.20
  twin  hollow  "[1@2 5@2 6@4 4@2 3@2 2@2 0@2]/4"               | vel 0.60 | lpf 2800 | reverb 0.28 | pan -0.22
  mirror thread "[5@2 1@2 0@4 2@2 3@2 4@2 6@2]/4"    @ 5..      | vel 0.45 | lpf 3200 | reverb 0.30 | pan 0.45
  pulse thread  "0 4 6 4"                            @ 9..      | oct -1 | vel 0.38 | lpf 3000 | reverb 0.20 | pan -0.40
  clock chime   "<4 6 2 4>(3,7)"                                | oct 1 | vel 0.32 | reverb 0.60 | pan -0.45

  group kit {
    drum skin  "X ~ x ~"                                        | vel 0.60 | pan 0.00
    hats hihat "x*8"                                 @ 5..      | vel 0.26 | pan 0.40
    tick rim   "~ ~ ~ x"                             @ 7..      | vel 0.38 | pan 0.50
  } | lpf 7000 | gain 0.88
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
--  IX. veil — C lydian, 4/4, 23s.
--     The theme loses its second half: head, then two bars of nothing.
--     The pad closes from 2.4 kHz to 700 Hz across the section and the
--     air comes back over the seam.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

section veil 8 {
  bpm 84
  sig 4/4
  scale C lydian

  pedal moor   "c2@3 ~@1"                                       | gain 0.85 | pan -0.10
  air   haze   "[g3,d4,e4]@2 [g3,d4,f#4]@2"                     | gain 0.55 | reverb 0.62 | lpf 2400..700 | ramp 8 exp | pan 0.15
  ring  glass  "[1@2 5@2 6@4 ~@8]/4"                            | oct 1 | vel 0.50 | reverb 0.55 | delay 0.714 0.30 0.28 | pan 0.20
  clock chime  "<4 6 2 4>(3,7)"                      @ ..6      | oct 1 | vel 0.24 | reverb 0.65 | pan -0.45
  drum  skin   "X ~ ~ ~"                             @ ..4      | vel 0.38 | pan 0.00
  wash  dust   "x/2"                                 @ 5..      | vel 0.26 | gain 0.40 | reverb 0.60 | lpf 2600..500 | ramp 8 exp
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
--  X. gate — G major, 4/4 at 56, 34s. The only tempo change in the
--     piece, and it is a modulation rather than a slowing: the new
--     eighth is the old triplet eighth.
--
--     The pad plays the same three notes it has played since bar 3 —
--     G, D, E and G, D, F# — but the drone under it is now G, so the
--     chord that was Cmaj9 and then a lydian #11 is heard as G6 and
--     Gmaj7. Nothing about the harmony has changed except what it is
--     standing on. That is the whole argument of the piece in one bar.
--
--     The theme keeps only its rise, 1 5 6, and is allowed for the first
--     and only time to finish: F# to G, the sharp resolving as a leading
--     tone, which is precisely what C lydian never let it do.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

section gate 8 {
  bpm 56
  sig 4/4
  scale G major

  pedal moor   "g1@3 ~@1"                                       | gain 0.95 | pan -0.10
  air   haze   "[[g3,d4,e4]@4 [g3,d4,f#4]@4 [g3,d4,e4]@4 [g3,d4,b4]@4]/4"  | gain 0.62 | reverb 0.60 | pan 0.15
  ring  glass  "[1@4 5@4 6@6 7@2]/4"                            | vel 0.60 | reverb 0.50 | delay 1.071 0.28 0.24 | pan 0.20
  twin  hollow "[1@4 5@4 6@6 7@2]/4"                 @ 5..      | oct -1 | vel 0.45 | lpf 2000 | reverb 0.30 | pan -0.20
  clock chime  "<4 6 2 4>(3,7)"                      @ ..4      | vel 0.26 | reverb 0.65 | pan -0.45
  low   thread "0 ~ ~ ~"                             @ 3..      | oct -1 | vel 0.35 | lpf 1600 | reverb 0.40 | pan -0.40
}

arrange dawn drift shade crux press solo flight press crest veil gate
